p('<b>Research finding.</b> The project separates three fundamentally different outcomes: a failed sampled Lyapunov inequality with an explicit counterexample, a passed finite state grid without a proof, and a sufficient all-state certificate within a mathematically specified neighborhood.')
small('The cover is an abstract conceptual visualization, not a measured closed-loop stability plot. This research does not certify a real robot or plant.')
page()
head('1   The industrial and scientific use case')
p('Control engineers and automated design systems can produce many possible state-feedback laws for a nonlinear plant. Before sending those laws to expensive simulation, experiment or hardware-in-the-loop testing, a screening component can evaluate the closed-loop state equations, actuator limits and Lyapunov-function derivative. A candidate that violates a condition at a known state can be rejected or reconsidered. A candidate that passes every sampled state is merely not yet falsified, because the region between samples remains untested.')
p('This paper investigates one precisely defined part of that workflow. Original paceval Mathematical Engine graph handles evaluate all explicit control, dynamics, Lyapunov and constraint formulas. A C++ driver generates synthetic candidates and state points and performs independent Runge-Kutta orchestration. An analytically derived sufficient inequality supplies an additional, conservative continuous-state result for a restricted two-state plant. Independently written C++ and 90-decimal-place Python references verify actual numerical results.')
sub('A concrete hypothetical example')
p('A laboratory robotics researcher considers two internal control states, one proportional to a normalized position deviation and another to a normalized coupled error. A generator proposes linear feedback gains, possibly enhanced with a bounded smooth nonlinear term. Before further investigation, the researcher tests whether the commanded input remains within an illustrative limit and whether a quadratic energy-like function decreases. The present experiment provides exactly that preliminary function screening, not a tested actuator or validated robotic dynamics model.')
sub('What is recorded')
tbl([['Evidence','Actual original-SDK result'],['Created and executed domain graphs',str(E['unique_graphs_executed'])+' / '+str(E['unique_graphs_created'])],['Feedback candidates',str(E['candidate_count'])+' distinct deterministic synthetic sets'],['Two model versions',str(E['model_candidate_combinations'])+' candidate/model combinations'],['Disk grid',str(E['sample_points_per_candidate'])+' points per candidate and version'],['Independent high-precision controls',f"{H['independent_checks']:,}"],['Independent analytic-condition controls',f"{C['checks']:,}"]],[CW*.44,CW*.56])
page()
head('2   Current research, tools and industrial boundaries')
p('Lyapunov theory remains central to the analysis of nonlinear closed-loop stability. Learning-based synthesis has broadened the set of feedback and certificate functions under investigation, while increasing the importance of separating candidate generation from mathematically sound verification. Yang et al. at ICML 2024 study stability-certified neural control policies with enlarged regions of attraction [1]. Debauche et al. in 2025 describe a counterexample-guided synthesis approach with formal checking for switched systems [2]. Devadze et al. in 2025 investigate computer-assisted certified SOS Lyapunov results for polynomial systems [3].')
p('The dReal tool supports nonlinear reasoning with explicitly different unsatisfiable and delta-satisfiable outcomes [4]. These approaches address proof search under their own assumptions and tool semantics. They are not implemented or benchmarked in this project. The present research uses an independently derived algebraic sufficient condition, plus direct graph-based calculation of every corresponding margin.')
sub('A controlled role for a formula engine')
p('The original paceval C/C++ API exposes persistent computation graphs and single or multi-graph numeric evaluation [5]. These capabilities fit repeated evaluation of changed coefficients and related formulas. In contrast, a formula evaluator does not infer a Lyapunov theorem, select a valid invariant set, or ensure the real physical plant satisfies the stated equations. The responsible host and research analyst retain those decisions. The result is an experimentally checkable division of mathematical execution and proof obligations.')
sub('Industrial integration questions')
p('A production controller would require validated system identification, measurement and actuator limits, digital sampling, numerical solver/quantization error analysis, faults and delays, robust margins for uncertainty, real-time software qualification and the applicable safety standards. No such industrial validation was attempted. The only demonstrated sufficient stability claim concerns the exact smooth two-state model and sublevel domain stated below.')
page()
head('3   Mathematical plant, two versions and Lyapunov function')
p('The initial WP 28 proposes a parameterized nonlinear feedback law, quadratic Lyapunov candidate, derivative and sampled residual. The extended demonstrator supplies a defined nonlinear continuous-time plant, two simultaneously addressable feedback versions, state and actuator limits, and a derivable sufficient condition. All plant data and feedback coefficients are synthetic normalized quantities.')
eq('dx/dt = -a*x + u + c*x*y^2')
eq('dy/dt = -b*y + d*x^2*y')
eq('V1: u = -k1*x - k2*y')
eq('V2: u = -k1*x - k2*y - k3*tanh(x),   k3 >= 0')
eq('V = p1*x^2 + p2*y^2,    p1,p2 > 0')
eq('Vdot = 2*p1*x*(dx/dt) + 2*p2*y*(dy/dt)')
p('The parameters a and b represent synthetic linear dissipative terms; c and d define nonlinear state coupling. V1 is purely linear feedback in the nonlinear plant. V2 adds a smooth saturation-like function of the first state, but the actuator itself is **not** clipped. A real actuator with hard saturation would create a different closed-loop vector field and require renewed analysis.')
sub('Main sample-level functions')
eq('m(x,y) = -eps*(x^2+y^2) - Vdot')
eq('State margin = R^2 - x^2 - y^2')
eq('Actuator margin = umax - abs(u)')
p('A sampled residual m below zero is a concrete counterexample to the chosen strict Lyapunov decrease condition at that state. A nonnegative residual at all inspected points does not by itself constrain any uninspected points. The second part of the experiment introduces a distinct mathematical sufficient test covering the entire specified region.')
page()
head('4   The analytic sufficient certificate')
p('Choose the closed disk D of points with x² + y² <= R², where 0 < R <= 1 and eps > 0. The quadratic candidate V is strictly positive except at the origin. For any |x| <= R <= 1, the elementary inequality x*tanh(x) >= (1 - R²/3)*x² holds. Cauchy/Young inequalities give 2|xy| <= x² + y², and the quartic coupling satisfies x²*y² <= R²*(x²+y²)/4. These bounds hold continuously, not merely at sampled grid points.')
p('With B=max(0,p1*c+p2*d), and k3*=0 for V1 or k3*=k3 for V2, define:')
eq('Mx = 2*p1*(a+k1) + 2*p1*k3star*(1-R^2/3)')
eq('      - p1*abs(k2) - B*R^2/2 - eps')
eq('My = 2*p2*b - p1*abs(k2) - B*R^2/2 - eps')
p('If both coefficient margins are nonnegative then, throughout the disk, Vdot <= -eps*(x²+y²). By the quadratic upper and lower bounds on V, the sublevel set Omega = {V <= min(p1,p2)*R²} lies within D, is forward invariant and provides a sufficient local exponential-stability conclusion for the mathematically specified continuous-time model.')
sub('A separate actuator guarantee')
eq('Mact = umax - R*(abs(k1)+abs(k2)+k3*)')
p('The conservative condition Mact >= 0 bounds the unmodified commanded control within the entire disk; requiring it alongside Mx and My is stricter than a sampled actuator test. The Boolean graph CERT_GUARD evaluates the conjunction with parameter positivity. The host interprets this flag without treating it as a certificate for any unmodeled actuator hardware.')
sub('The crucial logical distinction')
p('This test is **sufficient but not necessary**. When Mx or My is negative, the derivation does not establish the desired property; it does not establish instability. Even a negative sampled residual only falsifies the chosen Lyapunov inequality and may coexist with other, more suitable Lyapunov certificates.')
page()
head('5   Paceval execution architecture')
p('All substantive scalar formulas and composed final values remain actual original paceval graphs. The C++ host owns immutable formula versions, persistent handle lifetime, input schema checks, state grids, selection, RK4 time integration and file output. Each RK4 right-hand-side call itself invokes the original paceval closed-loop flow graphs. The multi-graph batch layout is verified by a probe with independently known values before any research data are processed.')
tbl([['Mathematical function / purpose','Actual executor','Graph family or rationale'],['Versioned feedback and both state derivatives','paceval','CONTROL; FLOW_X; FLOW_Y'],['Lyapunov candidate and complete Lie derivative','paceval','LYAPUNOV; VDOT'],['Pointwise decrease, state and actuator margins','paceval','RESIDUAL; STATE_MARGIN; ACTUATOR_MARGIN'],['Uniform coefficient bounds and actuator limit','paceval','M1_CERT; M2_CERT; CERT_MARGIN; GLOBAL_ACT_MARGIN'],['Mathematical Boolean and scored constraints','paceval','SAMPLED_GUARD; CERT_GUARD; COMPOSITE_SCORE'],['Analytic gain sensitivities','paceval','SENS_K1_VDOT; SENS_K2_VDOT; SENS_K3_VDOT'],['Between-version model differences','paceval','COMPARE_DELTA_*; COMPARE_BOTH_CERT'],['Candidate enumeration and CSV validation','C++ host','Stateful orchestration and strict numeric literals'],['RK4 integration and status interpretation','C++ host','Time sequencing with original paceval RHS']], [CW*.38,CW*.21,CW*.41])
p('The machine-readable graph inventory and execution evidence are included with source code. An executed graph is not counted merely because it was constructed. The original scalar API, batched multi-graph API and SDK numeric string-conversion routines are all exercised. The external original Linux library is not redistributed.')
page()
head('6   Inputs, reproducible test design and independent checks')
p('A deterministic seed generates 192 distinct parameter sets, including deliberately destabilizing feedback gains, multiple cross-coupling strengths and the two-version nonlinear gain. Both versions are evaluated at the same 317-point uniform grid within the unit disk. Every CSV row must follow a strictly validated decimal numeric literal grammar before being passed to the original paceval string-to-double routine. Source columns, order and units appear in the bundled schema document.')
p('The original program emits a complete `screening.csv` per candidate and version, representative `state_samples.csv` batches, model-comparison data, closed-loop trajectories, native crosschecks, a graph formula manifest, and an explicit mathematical function-execution inventory. A JSON evidence file supplies counts and the declared API paths. Figures are generated directly from these original-result CSV files rather than by re-evaluating the mathematics in the plotting script.')
sub('Independent numerical control')
p('The native C++ reference independently computes the feedback, Lyapunov candidate, Lie derivative and sufficient certificate coefficient margins for selected states and candidates. Python mpmath separately derives complete high-precision end values from the original input numbers and compares them against the recorded genuine paceval outputs. An independent mathematical-certifier checker tests the sufficient coefficient inequalities and the stated hyperbolic-tangent bound, and flags any supposed certificate contradicted by a sampled-state violation. Native and high-precision detailed discrepancies are recorded only in technical audit files.')
tbl([['Validation instrument','Executed result'],['Original paceval mathematical graphs',f"{E['unique_graphs_executed']} of {E['unique_graphs_created']}"],['Original native C++ end-value assertions',f"{E['native_endvalue_assertions']:,} passed"],['90-digit independent mpmath controls',f"{H['independent_checks']:,} passed"],['Independent analytic-certificate controls',f"{C['checks']:,} passed"],['Sampled certificates contradicted by a sampled failure',f"{C['false_certificates']}"]],[CW*.65,CW*.35])
page()
head('7   Main results: sampling versus sufficient proof')
p('The main experiment applies the same synthetic candidate parameters to a linear-feedback V1 model and an enhanced nonlinear-feedback V2 model. Each screen assesses the original paceval Lyapunov residual and actuator constraints. A separate stronger condition checks the analytic coefficient bounds and a conservative entire-disk actuator command limit. All reported counts originate from the original executed results and independently verified certificate audit.')
tbl([['Result class','V1','V2'],['Candidate laws','192','192'],['Pass all 317 sampled states',str(E['sampled_pass_V1']),str(E['sampled_pass_V2'])],['Satisfy full sufficient analytic + actuator test',str(E['analytic_certificate_V1']),str(E['analytic_certificate_V2'])],['At least one sampled counterexample',str(E['counterexample_candidate_V1']),str(E['counterexample_candidate_V2'])]],[CW*.56,CW*.22,CW*.22])
p('The nonlinear feedback variant satisfies the sampled screening condition for more of these particular synthetic candidates. It also satisfies the sufficient analytical criterion for more candidates under the selected parameter ranges. This result is neither an optimal control synthesis claim nor evidence of superiority on a physical system. It illustrates how two maintained mathematical formula versions change a reproducible control-analysis decision.')
sub('Why a more conservative test rejects additional cases')
p('A sampled success does not demonstrate all-state negativity or an entire-disk command bound. The sufficient test uses worst-case inequalities, so it rejects some state-grid-passing cases without proving that those cases are unstable. This conservative rejection is an expected, mathematically meaningful feature of the method.')
page()
