head('13   Sensitivities and compositional mathematical execution')
p('The application does not pretend that the current SDK automatically differentiates arbitrary expressions. It constructs analytically derived expressions directly as persistent original paceval graphs. For the demonstrated plant, the main Lyapunov derivative sensitivities with respect to controller gains are simple to state:')
eq('d(Vdot)/dk1 = -2*p1*x^2')
eq('d(Vdot)/dk2 = -2*p1*x*y')
eq('d(Vdot)/dk3 = 0 (V1);  -2*p1*x*tanh(x) (V2)')
p('The full graph-computed control is passed inside the full graph-computed Lie derivative. Separately, the sufficient coefficient margins are derived from the model parameters and evaluated in paceval; the host merely reads and interprets the resulting Boolean conjunction. Cross-version graph handles continue to be simultaneously available. The reference path recreates the underlying mathematics in native C++ or independent mpmath code, not by asking the paceval parser to interpret stored graph expressions again.')
sub('An auditable execution chain')
p('Validated CSV parameters → original paceval control and state functions → original paceval V, Vdot and margins → original paceval analytic coefficient and actuator guards → C++ sampled/review/certified interpretation → machine-readable original output and independent reference checks.')
p('The graph inventory also records state generation, numerical integration and decision logic explicitly as host responsibilities. Graph count is therefore not used as a surrogate for scientific correctness. Every important published result can be followed back through the actual source and saved result files.')
page()
head('14   Evidence limitations and next scientific steps')
p('The input parameters represent a synthetic family, not an identified real robot or industrial process. The Lyapunov theorem concerns a precisely specified smooth autonomous two-state system under its domain assumptions. It does not validate hardware sensing, actuator saturation or hysteresis, controller sampling, communication delays, disturbances, modeling uncertainty, mechanical safety, process qualification, or functional safety regulation.')
p('A larger positive analytic certificate margin is not a quantified safety reserve against arbitrary disturbances. The quadratic Lyapunov candidate and the Young-type inequality are conservative; a failed certificate is not a proof that the controller is unstable. A finite integration trace is neither a counterexample to an entire domain condition nor a global theorem. Finite mpmath and SDK pointwise tests provide numerical validation only; the sufficient proof is derived algebraically under the declared model assumptions.')
sub('Reasonable follow-on research')
p('The next experimental phase could introduce an identified nonlinear plant with measured noise and actuator limits, uncertainty envelopes, and a compatible robust Lyapunov candidate. A separate verifier such as SMT/dReal or polynomial SOS may be considered for candidates with assumptions it can actually represent. State and time discretization, sample-and-hold implementation, observability, and certification of the entire embedded control system require independent evidence. Nothing in the present execution is a CPU, GPU, FPGA, ASIC, energy-efficiency or latency benchmark.')
sub('Release and provenance checks')
p('The project contains original C++ SDK interfaces with their notices, the CMake source build, independent validators, reproducible CSV inputs and outputs, full mathematical inventories and ten figures in both PNG and vector PDF. The original shared library is an external dependency. Release checks include compiled real-SDK CTests, Markdown parser and negative-regression checks, full-PDF rendering and archive-integrity validation. Actual test status is recorded in technical result files rather than presumed from this publication.')
page()
